early one fourth of the old city's population still resides within the fort. It is also known as the Sonar Quila or Golden Fort because of the massive yellow sandstone walls. \n</p>\nMughals controlled the fort from 1541 till 1762. (Wikipedia)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2019-11-12 10:17","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/37583176@N00\">Ninara</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"160082305":{"pageid":160082305,"ns":6,"title":"File:Jaisalmer Fort, India (49244514282).jpg","index":6,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":7512827,"width":4743,"height":3162,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg/1920px-Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1920,"thumbheight":1280,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg/3840px-Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg/1920px-Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1920,"height":1280,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg/3840px-Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6e/Jaisalmer_Fort%2C_India_%2849244514282%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaisalmer_Fort,_India_(49244514282).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=160082305","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Rajasthan\nLegend has it that the fort was built by Rawal Jaisal, a Bhati Rajput, in 1156 CE. It is believed to be one of the very few \"living forts\" in the world (such as Carcassonne, France), as nearly one fourth of the old city's population still resides within the fort. It is also known as the Sonar Quila or Golden Fort because of the massive yellow sandstone walls. \n</p>\nMughals controlled the fort from 1541 till 1762. (Wikipedia)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2019-11-12 11:05","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/37583176@N00\">Ninara</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY 2.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}},"mime":"image/jpeg"}]},"21115354":{"pageid":21115354,"ns":6,"title":"File:Jaisalmer Fort after entering the boundary.JPG","index":3,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":1745533,"width":2592,"height":1944,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Jaisalmer_Fort_after_entering_the_boundary.JPG/1920px-Jaisalmer_Fort_after_entering_the_boundary.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1920,"thumbheight":1440,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Jaisalmer_Fort_after_entering_the_boundary.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Jaisalmer_Fort_after_entering_the_boundary.JPG/1920px-Jaisalmer_Fort_after_entering_the_boun